ass="nav"><div class="nav-inner"><a href="index.html" style="display:flex;align-items:center;gap:8px;text-decoration:none"><svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" style="color:var(--emerald)"><path d="M12 2L2 7l10 5 10-5-10-5z"/><path d="M2 17l10 5 10-5"/><path d="M2 12l10 5 10-5"/></svg><span class="brand-text">vlymbooq</span></a><div class="nav-links"><a href="math.html">Math Map</a><a href="physics.html">Physics Map</a></div></div></nav><div class="container"><div class="lesson-body"><h2>Atomic Physics — Atomic Structure, Bohr Model, Spectra &amp; Fine Structure</h2><p>Deep dive into Atomic Physics — the structure of atoms, <strong>Rutherford's nuclear model</strong> (a dense positively charged nucleus surrounded by electrons), the <strong>Bohr model</strong> of the hydrogen atom (quantized angular momentum, discrete energy levels E_n = −13.6/n² eV, and transitions between levels), <strong>quantum numbers</strong> (n, ℓ, m_ℓ, m_s), <strong>atomic orbitals</strong> (s, p, d, f shapes and their radial and angular wavefunctions), <strong>atomic spectra</strong> (the Lyman, Balmer, and Paschen series), <strong>electron configuration</strong> (the Aufbau principle, the Pauli exclusion principle, Hund's rules), <strong>spin-orbit coupling</strong> (the L·S interaction and fine structure splitting), <strong>hyperfine structure</strong> (the interaction of electron and nuclear spins), the <strong>Zeeman effect</strong> (splitting of spectral lines in a magnetic field: normal and anomalous), and the <strong>Stark effect</strong> (splitting in an electric field).</p><div class="quote-box"><div class="quote">"All of physics is either impossible or trivial. It is impossible until you understand it, and then it becomes trivial."</div><div class="attribution">— Ernest Rutherford</div></div><div class="section-label part1">Part 1</div><h2>⚛️ Atomic Structure and the Bohr Model</h2><div class="story-card"><div class="story-label">Early Models</div><div class="story-title">Rutherford's Nuclear Model, Bohr's Postulates, and Hydrogenic Atoms</div><div class="story-text">The <strong>Rutherford gold foil experiment</strong> (1909) revealed that atoms consist of a small, dense, positively charged nucleus surrounded by electrons. The <strong>Bohr model</strong> (1913) postulated: (1) electrons orbit the nucleus in circular orbits without radiating energy (stationary states), (2) angular momentum is quantized: mvr = nℏ with n = 1, 2, 3, …, (3) electrons can transition between orbits by absorbing or emitting a photon of energy hν = E_i − E_f. The <strong>Bohr radius</strong> a₀ = 4πε₀ℏ²/(m_e e²) = 0.529 Å. The <strong>energy levels</strong> of hydrogen: E_n = −(m_e e⁴)/(32π²ε₀²ℏ²) · 1/n² = −13.6 eV / n². The <strong>Rydberg constant</strong> R_H = 1.097 × 10⁷ m⁻¹, giving the spectral formula 1/λ = R_H (1/n_f² − 1/n_i²). The <strong>Lyman series</strong> (n_f = 1, ultraviolet), <strong>Balmer series</strong> (n_f = 2, visible), <strong>Paschen series</strong> (n_f = 3, infrared). The <strong>Sommerfeld model</strong> introduced elliptical orbits and the fine-structure constant α = e²/(4πε₀ℏc) ≈ 1/137, accounting for relativistic corrections and the azimuthal quantum number ℓ. The <strong>quantum mechanical hydrogen atom</strong> solves the Schrödinger equation in spherical coordinates, yielding the wavefunctions ψ_{nℓm}(r, θ, φ) = R_{nℓ}(r) Y_ℓ^m(θ, φ) where R_{nℓ} are associated Laguerre functions and Y_ℓ^m are spherical harmonics.</div><div class="story-moral">The Bohr model introduced quantization of angular momentum, explaining the discrete energy levels and spectral lines of hydrogen; the full quantum treatment provides the exact wavefunctions and orbital shapes.</div></div><div class="section-label part2">Part 2</div><h2>🔬 Quantum Numbers and Atomic Orbitals</h2><div class="story-card"><div class="story-label">Orbitals</div><div class="story-title">The Four Quantum Numbers, Orbital Shapes, and the Pauli Exclusion Principle</div><div class="story-text">Electrons in atoms are described by four <strong>quantum numbers</strong>: the <strong>principal quantum number</strong> n = 1, 2, 3, … (determines energy and radial extent), the <strong>azimuthal quantum number</strong> ℓ = 0, 1, …, n−1 (determines orbital angular momentum and shape: ℓ = 0 is s, ℓ = 1 is p, ℓ = 2 is d, ℓ = 3 is f), the <strong>magnetic quantum number</strong> m_ℓ = −ℓ, …, ℓ (determines orientation in space), and the <strong>spin quantum number</strong> m_s = ±1/2 (electron spin). The <strong>Pauli exclusion principle</strong>: no two electrons can have the same set of all four quantum numbers — each orbital (n, ℓ, m_ℓ) can hold at most two electrons with opposite spins. The <strong>radial wavefunctions</strong> R_{nℓ}(r) have (n−ℓ−1) nodes; the 1s orbital is nodeless and exponential, R_{10} = 2 a₀^{-3/2} e^{-r/a₀}. The angular part |Y_ℓ^m|² gives characteristic shapes: s orbitals are spherical, p orbitals are dumbbell-shaped (p_x, p_y, p_z), d orbitals have four-lobed cloverleaf shapes (d_{xy}, d_{xz}, d_{yz}, d_{x²−y²}, d_{z²}). The <strong>probability density</strong> |ψ|² gives the electron cloud. The <strong>radial distribution function</strong> P(r) = r² |R_{nℓ}(r)|² gives the probability of finding an electron at distance r from the nucleus. For hydrogen, the most probable radius for the 1s state is exactly a₀.</div><div class="story-moral">The four quantum numbers completely specify an electron's state; orbital shapes emerge from the angular part of the wavefunction; the Pauli principle governs the filling of atomic shells.</div></div><div class="section-label part3">Part 3</div><h2>📋 Electron Configuration and the Periodic Table</h2><div class="story-card"><div class="story-label">Configuration</div><div class="story-title">Aufbau Principle, Hund's Rules, and the Structure of the Periodic Table</div><div class="story-text">The <strong>Aufbau principle</strong>: electrons fill orbitals in order of increasing energy: 1s, 2s, 2p, 3s, 3p, 4s, 3d, 4p, 5s, 4d, 5p, 6s, 4f, 5d, 6p, 7s, 5f, 6d, 7p. The <strong>n+ℓ rule</strong>: orbitals with lower n+ℓ are filled first; for equal n+ℓ, lower n comes first (e.g., 4s fills before 3d because 4+0=4 < 3+2=5). <strong>Hund's rules</strong> (for ground state of a given configuration): (1) maximize total spin S (Hund's first rule — minimizes exchange energy), (2) maximize total orbital angular momentum L consistent with S (Hund's second rule — minimizes electrostatic repulsion), (3) for less-than-half-filled shells, J = |L−S|; for more-than-half-filled, J = L+S (spin-orbit coupling). <strong>Electron configuration notation</strong>: e.g., Fe (Z=26) = [Ar] 3d⁶ 4s². The <strong>periodic table</strong> is organized by electron configuration: groups correspond to similar outer shell configurations, periods correspond to principal quantum number n. <strong>Transition metals</strong> involve filling of d orbitals; <strong>lanthanides and actinides</strong> involve filling of f orbitals. <strong>Ionization energy</strong> (the energy required to remove an electron) shows periodic trends, increasing across a period and decreasing down a group. <strong>Electron affinity</strong> and <strong>electronegativity</strong> (the power of an atom to attract electrons in a bond) also exhibit periodic behavior described by the Pauling scale.</div><div class="story-moral">The Aufbau principle and Hund's rules determine electron configurations, which in turn determine chemical properties and the structure of the periodic table.</div></div><div class="section-label part4">Part 4</div><h2>🌀 Fine Structure, Hyperfine Structure, and External Fields</h2><div class="story-card"><div class="story-label">Splitting</div><div class="story-title">Spin-Orbit Coupling, Fine Structure, Hyperfine Splitting, and the Zeeman Effect</div><div class="story-text"><strong>Spin-orbit coupling</strong> (the L·S interaction) arises from the interaction of the electron's magnetic moment with the magnetic field generated by its orbital motion. The fine-structure Hamiltonian: H_{SO} = ξ(r) L·S, where ξ(r) = (1/(2m_e²c²)) (1/r) dV/dr. For hydrogen, the fine-structure splitting ΔE_{FS} = (α²/2n³) (1/(j+1/2) − 3/(4n)) Ry, where j = ℓ ± 1/2 is the total angular momentum quantum number. The fine-structure constant α = e²/(4πε₀ℏc) ≈ 1/137 sets the scale. The <strong>hyperfine structure</strong> arises from the interaction of the electron's total angular momentum J with the nuclear spin I: H_{hf} = A I·J, where A is the hyperfine coupling constant. The classic example: the 21 cm (1420 MHz) hydrogen line from the hyperfine transition of ground-state hydrogen. The <strong>Zeeman effect</strong>: splitting of spectral lines in an external magnetic field B. The <strong>normal Zeeman effect</strong> (for singlet states, S=0): ΔE = μ_B B Δm_ℓ, splitting into three components (the Lorentz triplet). The <strong>anomalous Zeeman effect</strong> (for S≠0): ΔE = μ_B g_J B m_J, where g_J = 1 + [j(j+1) + s(s+1) − ℓ(ℓ+1)]/[2j(j+1)] is the <strong>Landé g-factor</strong>. The <strong>Paschen-Back effect</strong>: in very strong magnetic fields, the L·S coupling breaks down, and the Zeeman pattern simplifies. The <strong>Stark effect</strong>: splitting in an external electric field. The <strong>linear Stark effect</strong> (hydrogen only, due to degeneracy) shifts levels by ΔE = 3ea₀n(n₁−n₂)E; other atoms exhibit the <strong>quadratic Stark effect</strong> ΔE ∝ E².</div><div class="story-moral">Spin-orbit coupling produces fine-structure splitting of spectral lines; hyperfine structure reveals nuclear properties; the Zeeman and Stark effects probe the response of atoms to external fields.</div></div><div class="section-divider"></div><h2 style="margin-top:0">🎯 Practice</h2><div id="practice-qs"></div><div style="margin-top:16px"><button class="btn btn-primary" onclick="resetPractice()">🔄 Reset All</button><a href="physics.html" class="btn btn-success">← Back to Physics Map</a></div></div></div><script>
var practiceQs=[{id:45097,text:"⚛️ The Bohr model postulates that angular momentum is quantized as:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"mvr = nℏ",c:true},{l:"B",t:"mv²/r = nℏ",c:false},{l:"C",t:"E = nhν",c:false},{l:"D",t:"r = n²a₀",c:false}],sol:"Bohr's quantization condition: mvr = nℏ for n = 1, 2, 3, … This selects discrete orbits where the electron does not radiate."},{id:45098,text:"⚛️ The ground state energy of hydrogen in the Bohr model is:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"−13.6 eV",c:true},{l:"B",t:"−3.4 eV",c:false},{l:"C",t:"−1.51 eV",c:false},{l:"D",t:"−0.85 eV",c:false}],sol:"The <strong>ground state</strong> of hydrogen (n=1) has energy E₁ = −13.6 eV. Higher levels: E₂ = −3.4 eV, E₃ = −1.51 eV, E₄ = −0.85 eV."},{id:45099,text:"⚛️ The Balmer series corresponds to transitions ending at n_f =:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"2",c:true},{l:"B",t:"1",c:false},{l:"C",t:"3",c:false},{l:"D",t:"∞",c:false}],sol:"The <strong>Balmer series</strong> has n_f = 2 (transitions to the first excited state), producing visible spectral lines. Lyman: n_f = 1, Paschen: n_f = 3."},{id:45100,text:"🔬 The azimuthal quantum number ℓ for a d orbital is:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"2",c:true},{l:"B",t:"0",c:false},{l:"C",t:"1",c:false},{l:"D",t:"3",c:false}],sol:"For <strong>d orbitals</strong>, ℓ = 2. The letters correspond to ℓ: s=0, p=1, d=2, f=3."},{id:45101,text:"🔬 How many electrons can occupy a single orbital (given by n, ℓ, m_ℓ)?",opts:[{l:"A",t:"2",c:true},{l:"B",t:"1",c:false},{l:"C",t:"8",c:false},{l:"D",t:"6",c:false}],sol:"Due to the <strong>Pauli exclusion principle</strong>, each orbital can hold at most two electrons with opposite spins (m_s = ±1/2)."},{id:45102,text:"🔬 The probability density of an electron is given by:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"|ψ|²",c:true},{l:"B",t:"ψ",c:false},{l:"C",t:"|ψ|",c:false},{l:"D",t:"ψ²",c:false}],sol:"The <strong>probability density</strong> is |ψ|² = ψ* ψ. The radial distribution function is P(r) = r²|R_{nℓ}(r)|²."},{id:45103,text:"📋 According to the Aufbau principle, which orbital fills before 3d?",opts:[{l:"A",t:"4s",c:true},{l:"B",t:"4p",c:false},{l:"C",t:"3p",c:false},{l:"D",t:"4d",c:false}],sol:"The <strong>Aufbau principle</strong> with the n+ℓ rule: 4s (n+ℓ=4) fills before 3d (n+ℓ=5)."},{id:45104,text:"📋 Hund's first rule states that the ground state maximizes:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Total spin S",c:true},{l:"B",t:"Total orbital angular momentum L",c:false},{l:"C",t:"Total angular momentum J",c:false},{l:"D",t:"Energy",c:false}],sol:"<strong>Hund's first rule</strong>: maximize total spin S (this minimizes exchange energy, keeping electrons parallel and separated)."},{id:45105,text:"📋 Iron (Fe, Z=26) has the electron configuration:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"[Ar] 3d⁶ 4s²",c:true},{l:"B",t:"[Ar] 3d⁸",c:false},{l:"C",t:"[Ne] 3d⁶ 4s²",c:false},{l:"D",t:"[Kr] 3d⁶",c:false}],sol:"<strong>Iron</strong> (Z=26) has configuration [Ar] 3d⁶ 4s². Argon core = 1s²2s²2p⁶3s²3p⁶."},{id:45106,text:"🌀 The fine-structure constant α is approximately:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"1/137",c:true},{l:"B",t:"1/100",c:false},{l:"C",t:"1/1000",c:false},{l:"D",t:"1/2",c:false}],sol:"The <strong>fine-structure constant</strong> α = e²/(4πε₀ℏc) ≈ 1/137. It characterizes the strength of the electromagnetic interaction and sets the scale of atomic fine structure."},{id:45107,text:"🌀 In the anomalous Zeeman effect, the Landé g-factor for an electron state is:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"g_J = 1 + [j(j+1) + s(s+1) − ℓ(ℓ+1)]/[2j(j+1)]",c:true},{l:"B",t:"g_J = 1",c:false},{l:"C",t:"g_J = 2",c:false},{l:"D",t:"g_J = 0",c:false}],sol:"The <strong>Landé g-factor</strong> accounts for the spin contribution to the magnetic moment. For S=0, g_J = 1 (normal Zeeman); for ℓ=0, g_J ≈ 2 (spin only)."},{id:45108,text:"🌀 The 21 cm hydrogen line arises from:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Hyperfine splitting of ground-state hydrogen",c:true},{l:"B",t:"The Lyman alpha transition",c:false},{l:"C",t:"Fine-structure splitting of the n=2 level",c:false},{l:"D",t:"The Balmer alpha line",c:false}],sol:"The <strong>21 cm line</strong> (1420 MHz) is the hyperfine transition of ground-state hydrogen — the spin-flip of the electron relative to the proton. It is crucial in radio astronomy."}];
